module gunbc.plans.fabric_serving_router

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { h2, h3, p, li, ul, ol, quote }
import gunbc.design_ruling { project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts }

fn fabric_serving_router_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    quote(blocks: [
      p(text: "**Status: plan, awaiting review. No code lands before this plan is reviewed.** It adds a fabric-native serving router so that dashboard agent sessions (OpenAI-compatible `/v1/completions` and `/v1/chat/completions`, streaming) can use flex-rented cards: Vast now, AWS EC2 later. Operator ruling 2026-10-23: the ctrl repository is throwaway and nothing may depend on it. Its router ranks replicas by running-request count, which `gunbc.serving.router_realization` `ComputeOwnLoadScore` forbids."),
      p(text: "**Evidence.** Main was read at cb6548946213 (2026-10-23). The gap analysis behind the brief was re-checked against that tree, and the corrections are in section 2."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "1. What already exists (the decision half)"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Contract.** `gunbc.serving.router_realization` `router_capability_permission`. The router may normalize a request to demand, ask the placement authority, take the endpoint from the grant, proxy bytes and release the seat. It may not compute its own load score, choose a host directly, keep an authoritative binding ledger, invent a placement when placement is unreadable, or forward a request without a permit."),
      li(text: "**Placement and release.** `gunbc.harness.harness_cli` `harness_place_cli` / `harness_release_cli`, over `gunbc.serving.turn_admission` `serving_group_admission`, `product.capacity` (lease, pool_events, redemption) and `product.fabric.selection` `select_supply`."),
      li(text: "**Front door.** `gunbc.serving.serving_front_door` (`front_door_admit_on_store`, `front_door_stream_end`, `front_door_recover`, `mint_serving_seat_permit`). Witnesses execute it, but nothing deploys it."),
      li(text: "**Release legs.** `gunbc.serving.rented_release` (drain, revoke, clean, destroy) as decisions, with no driver."),
      li(text: "**Enrollment derivation.** `gunbc.serving.rented_allocation_host_enrollment` `rented_allocation_host_enrollment`. Its consumer row, `gunbc.serving.rented_enrollment` `rented_cohort_enrollments`, is a hand-written list that is empty (`[]`) in production."),
      li(text: "**A native serve door (missing from the brief's analysis).** `std.compiler_entry` `NativeServeDriver` with its contract `gunbc.native_serve`. It is emitted from `.dag`; the rendered main comes from `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `emit_native_serve_driver_main_rs`. It is exercised end to end by `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:native-serve`, and the device-crypto verifier is already a production consumer (`gunbc.auth.approval_device_crypto_serve`). Its effect denominator, `std.compiler_entry` `native_service_route_denominator`, admits outgoing HTTP and the clock (ruling of 2026-10-04 recorded on `gunbc.plans.demand_engine_program`)."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "2. Corrections to the brief's gap analysis"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Gap 2: `extdeps.openrouter` is not the OpenAI wire.** It models OpenRouter's own key, credit and limit API. The chat and completions request/response shapes and the SSE event stream belong to a separately governed upstream (the OpenAI API reference), so they get their own module, `extdeps.openai`, under section 3's external upstream decomposition. OpenRouter is a consumer of that shape, not its authority."),
      li(text: "**Gap 1 has a v2 home already** (section 3). The question is not whether serving can leave the seed. It is how the work divides between that door and the component that carries the bytes."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "3. The load-bearing decision: where the streaming listener lives"),
    h3(text: "Option A: extend the seed's `gunbc serve`"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Where it would go: `v1_compiler.cli_run` `handle_serve` / `serve_read_request` / `serve_write_response`. Today that path reads one Content-Length request and writes one response, with no chunked encoding, no keep-alive and no TLS. Its serial accept loop also runs every request under the evaluator budget."),
      li(text: "Purpose test (`gunbc.v1.v1_maintenance_standing` `v1_seed_standing`): **fails.** A streaming proxy makes the seed more capable and does not move the v2 self-host frontier. That is `SeedFeatureCompletion`, plus `PublicSurfaceGrowth` on a seed verb. Both refused classes take precedence over any admission. The precedents that were admitted on this seam, `gunbc.serve_liveness_seed_growth` and `gunbc.fabric_door_socket_seed_growth`, were each argued as narrow operator-availability or principal-attestation obligations. Their own rows call the purpose admission the weak point. A streaming router is a product feature, so it is not one of those."),
      li(text: "It would also put a second serving door beside `NativeServeDriver`. That is the parallel authority that section 6 lists as a tell."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "Option B: bytes through an upstream proxy, decisions on the native door (decided, calm-crab-645 on gunbc#14929, 2026-10-23)"),
    p(text: "This combines this plan's first draft with stern-fox-207's gunbc#14927, which was closed as a duplicate lane. The first draft put a streaming relay arm in the native door's rendered main. That arm is withdrawn: a relay in the emitted main is a hand-built proxy in a Rust template. It re-invents an upstream we can model, and it cements Rust into the template, which DESIGN section 7 warns against."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Bytes** move through an upstream proxy, Envoy or OpenResty. Whichever is chosen is version-pinned in its own extdeps module (neither exists today), and its configuration is emitted from gunbc, never hand-written."),
      li(text: "**Decisions** are ordinary request/response handlers served by the v2 native serve door (`NativeServeDriver`), which already serves this shape. The **authz callout** normalizes the request to demand, asks placement, admits it at the front door and redeems the grant. It answers with the upstream endpoint and the seat permit, or a typed refusal. The **stream-end callout** calls `front_door_stream_end` and then releases the seat."),
      li(text: "**The proxy takes its upstream only from the authz answer.** No static upstreams, load balancing, retries or health-based routing. Each is disabled explicitly in the emitted configuration, and a witness checks that it is off. A second placement policy inside the proxy is exactly the `ComputeOwnLoadScore` / `SelectHostDirectly` fork."),
      li(text: "**An unreachable callout is a refusal.** If the authz callout is unreachable, the proxy fails closed and never forwards (`ForwardWithoutPermit`)."),
    ]),
    p(text: "**Falsifier first, and it chooses the proxy.** Before either proxy is adopted, a harness drives each candidate through these cases: normal end of stream, client disconnect, upstream reset and proxy restart. In every case the stream-end callout must fire or, where it is lost, `front_door_recover` or lease expiry must recover the seat. Envoy versus OpenResty is decided by that measurement, not by preference."),
    p(text: "**The named later realization.** A v2-native streaming door replaces the proxy when native emission can host the byte carriage itself, rather than in a hand-written Rust template. Until that capability exists, the proxy is the realization, not a scaffold."),

    h2(text: "4. Slices, in dependency order (one PR each, none merged by this lane)"),
    ol(items: [
      li(text: "**S1 Proxy falsifier and choice.** A harness that runs both candidates against a fake upstream that streams chunks, with stub callouts, and observes the cases in section 3. The result chooses Envoy or OpenResty. The chosen proxy gets its own pinned extdeps module, and its configuration is emitted, including the explicit off-switches for static upstreams, load balancing, retries and health routing, each witnessed."),
      li(text: "**S2 `extdeps.openai`.** The request and response records for chat completions and completions, the SSE `data:` / `[DONE]` framing, and the error envelope, cited to the upstream reference with its version declared. The authz callout reads only what it needs to normalize to demand: the model, a prompt-size reading and the `stream` flag. The proxy carries the body untouched."),
      li(text: "**S3 Callout entry.** A `NativeServeDriver` program, `gunbc.serving.serving_router`, serving the authz and stream-end callouts. The authz callout calls the `harness_place_cli` folds in-process, then admits, redeems and answers. It has no scoring, no host choice and no failover outside placement. An unreadable placement is a typed refusal (`FabricatePlacementWhenUnreadable` stays forbidden). A witness exercises the route against each `RouterCapability` arm. The S1 harness reruns against the real callouts."),
      li(text: "**S4 Client key.** A pool API key checked by the authz callout through `gunbc.auth.request_authentication` and issued under `gunbc.serving.serving_key_grant`. TLS stays tailnet-only. No key or endpoint appears in any PR."),
      li(text: "**S5 Rent-to-enroll actuation.** Replace the hand list `rented_cohort_enrollments` with durable state written by applying `rented_allocation_host_enrollment` to each live allocation, so an enrolled card can be placed. **Sequenced after gunbc#14879** (witty-lynx-354 owns rented_allocation and rented_release, and that PR makes them supplier-agnostic). This lane edits neither carrier while #14879 is open."),
      li(text: "**S6 Reclaim driver and session re-placement.** A driver that walks the `rented_release` legs in order (drain, revoke, clean, destroy) and stops at the first leg that refuses. Re-placing a session whose card is reclaimed has **no home today**. It is a modeling obligation that joins `gunbc.serving.replica_endpoint_launch`'s incarnation frontier: the session's next turn asks placement again and pays a cold prefill. It is not a router-side failover set, and no generic replica-failover abstraction is introduced (`gunbc.fabric.fabric_storage_placement` is one placement, not a replica set). The model lands before the driver. Destroy runs only after #14879's destroy-target fix."),
      li(text: "**S7 Live deploy.** Units and legs for the proxy and the callout service through `gunbc.live_deploy.apply_legs` and `gunbc.live_deploy.emit`, never a hand-written script."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "5. First live target"),
    p(text: "Vast, Apply on, a $50 spend cap and at most 2 cards, after #14879's destroy fix lands. One dashboard session streams a chat completion through the router to a rented card; the card is then reclaimed through S6, and the session's next turn is placed on the surviving card. RED for the design: any turn served without a permit, any seat still held after its stream ended, or any rented card still running after reclaim reported done."),
  ]
}

data fabric_serving_router_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly { ruling: project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts },
  slug: "fabric-serving-router",
  title: "Fabric-native serving router: proxy bytes, native-door decisions, OpenAI wire, rent-to-enroll and reclaim",
  body: fabric_serving_router_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen { condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Delete this plan when S1 to S7 are realized and their rules are carried by the serving, proxy extdeps and live_deploy modules' own declarations and witnesses.") },
}
